{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"files\":[{\"content\":\"#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n#include <string.h>\\n#include <math.h>\\n\\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\\n\\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\\n\\n/* Calculate one body's force in the original ascending source-body order. */\\nstatic void body_force_one(Body *p, float dt, int n, int i) {\\n  float fx = 0.0f;\\n  float fy = 0.0f;\\n  float fz = 0.0f;\\n\\n  for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\\n    float dx = p[j].x - p[i].x;\\n    float dy = p[j].y - p[i].y;\\n    float dz = p[j].z - p[i].z;\\n    float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\\n    float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\\n    float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\\n\\n    fx += dx * inv_dist3;\\n    fy += dy * inv_dist3;\\n    fz += dz * inv_dist3;\\n  }\\n\\n  p[i].vx += dt*fx;\\n  p[i].vy += dt*fy;\\n  p[i].vz += dt*fz;\\n}\\n\\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\\n  if (b != 1) {\\n    fprintf(stderr,\\\"\\\\nError reading nbodie value\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\\n  }\\n  Body *bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\\n  if (b != 1) {\\n    fprintf(stderr,\\\"\\\\nError reading input values\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\\n  }\\n\\n  return bodies;\\n}\\n\\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\\n  if (b != 1) {\\n    fprintf(stderr,\\\"\\\\nError writing to output\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nint main(int argc,char **argv) {\\n  int nbodies;\\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\\n\\n  /* Reuse one team; worksharing barriers separate force and position phases. */\\n  #pragma omp parallel\\n  {\\n    for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; iter++) {\\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\\n        body_force_one(bodies, DT, nbodies, i);\\n      }\\n\\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; i++) {\\n        bodies[i].x += bodies[i].vx * DT;\\n        bodies[i].y += bodies[i].vy * DT;\\n        bodies[i].z += bodies[i].vz * DT;\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\\n  free(bodies);\\n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\\n}\\n\",\"path\":\"src/nbody.c\"}],\"hypothesis\":\"A persistent OpenMP team avoids repeated team setup while static worksharing distributes independent target bodies; extracting the per-body force calculation preserves each target's original ascending-j arithmetic order. This omits the previously slower serial threshold and the unhelpful SIMD/tiling/compact-position variants.\",\"notes\":\"Full-program candidate for controller evaluation. The source preserves binary I/O and the force accumulation sequence; correctness and speed remain for the controller to assess.\",\"research\":{\"queries\":[],\"urls\":[]},\"sources_kept\":[\"a841149e87744055\",\"ae2aedf049404be5\"],\"transformations\":[\"Extract the per-body force calculation into a helper, retaining the original increasing-j accumulation order.\",\"Use one persistent OpenMP team across simulation iterations with static worksharing for force calculations and position updates.\"]}"}}
